     1	# Field Name Aliases in Schema-Once Output: Do They Save Tokens?
     2	
     3	**A compact formatter has already paid to say a field name once. Is an alias dictionary worth another schema lookup to save a few tokens?**
     4	
     5	*February 2026; evidence boundaries revised September 2026.*
     6	
     7	---
     8	
     9	## The question and its boundary
    10	
    11	A task-list formatter can return a header once and values beneath it:
    12	
    13	~~~text
    14	id,name,status
    15	T-209,Schema migration,development
    16	T-210,Release notes,to-review
    17	~~~
    18	
    19	This is the familiar baseline: field names identify the columns once, while the
    20	rows carry only values. Replacing the header with aliases such as
    21	<code>i,n,s</code> can make that one line shorter, but it also requires an
    22	agent to learn and retain the dictionary.
    23	
    24	This article asks a deliberately bounded question: **for this repository's
    25	schema-once compact formatter, do field aliases justify their discovery and
    26	comprehension cost?** It covers the checked-in alias fixtures, the associated
    27	session model, and the current Go implementation of compact output and DSL
    28	batching. It does not establish a token winner between the DSL and MCP, and it
    29	does not generalize the results to arbitrary tokenizers, hosts, or transports.
    30	
    31	The evidence types are kept distinct throughout:
    32	
    33	| Label | What it means here |
    34	| --- | --- |
    35	| **Verified implementation fact** | Current source and executable tests establish a behavior of this Go implementation. |
    36	| **Checked-in historical measurement** | A fixture, script, and recorded result exist, but this revision did not rerun the tokenizer. |
    37	| **Model estimate** | A simulator result depends on stated constants and eviction assumptions; it is not a runtime observation. |
    38	| **Reasoning** | A conditional design recommendation rather than a numeric result. |
    39	| **Unknown** | The repository has no aligned artifact that could support the claim. |
    40	
    41	The detailed classification and MCP claim dispositions are in the accepted
    42	[MCP token-economics evidence map](../.research/260923_mcp-token-economics-evidence.md).
    43	
    44	## Study 1: What one abbreviated header saves
    45	
    46	**Evidence: checked-in historical measurement.** The fixtures use task-tracker
    47	payloads at 5, 20, 100, and 500 items, each with eight fields:
    48	<code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>status</code>,
    49	<code>assignee</code>, <code>description</code>,
    50	<code>priority</code>, <code>created</code>, and <code>updated</code>.
    51	The checked-in [measurement script](../.research/synthetic-payloads/measure.py)
    52	tokenizes fixed JSON, compact-full, and compact-alias fixtures with
    53	<code>tiktoken</code> and <code>cl100k_base</code>. These are historical fixture
    54	results, not tokenizer counts re-attested by this revision.
    55	
    56	| Items | JSON | Compact-full | Compact-alias |
    57	| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
    58	| 5 | 485 | 269 | 264 |
    59	| 20 | 1,957 | 1,055 | 1,050 |
    60	| 100 | 9,836 | 5,283 | 5,278 |
    61	| 500 | 48,933 | 26,144 | 26,139 |
    62	
    63	The same recorded data makes the contrast explicit:
    64	
    65	| Transition | 5 items | 20 items | 100 items | 500 items |
    66	| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
    67	| JSON to compact-full | -44.5% | -46.1% | -46.3% | -46.6% |
    68	| Compact-full to compact-alias | -1.86% | -0.47% | -0.09% | -0.02% |
    69	| **Absolute alias saving** | **5 tok** | **5 tok** | **5 tok** | **5 tok** |
    70	
    71	The concrete example explains the fixed result. The full header
    72	
    73	~~~text
    74	id,name,status,assignee,description,priority,created,updated
    75	~~~
    76	
    77	is emitted once; the abbreviated version
    78	
    79	~~~text
    80	i,n,s,a,d,p,c,u
    81	~~~
    82	
    83	changes only that declaration. The value rows do not repeat either set of field
    84	names. The historical fixture counts therefore show a 5-token header delta at
    85	every tested payload size; they do not prove the same percentage or token count
    86	for another tokenizer or formatter.
    87	
    88	The per-item values below are another view of those same historical fixtures,
    89	not a new measurement:
    90	
    91	| Items | JSON tok/item | Compact-full tok/item | Compact-alias tok/item |
    92	| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
    93	| 5 | 97.0 | 53.8 | 52.8 |
    94	| 20 | 97.8 | 52.8 | 52.5 |
    95	| 100 | 98.4 | 52.8 | 52.8 |
    96	| 500 | 97.9 | 52.3 | 52.3 |
    97	
    98	## Study 2: What a remembered dictionary costs
    99	
   100	**Evidence: checked-in historical benchmark record.** The comprehension
   101	materials under [.research/comprehension-tests](../.research/comprehension-tests/)
   102	compare full names with aliases at three levels: 5, 15, and 30 fields. Each
   103	level has 12 data items and 10 questions spanning lookup, filtering,
   104	cross-reference, aggregation, and multi-field reasoning. The earlier benchmark
   105	reported the following result when an explicit dictionary was supplied:
   106	
   107	| Level | Fields | Abbreviated | Full | Delta |
   108	| --- | ---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
   109	| 1 | 5 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
   110	| 2 | 15 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
   111	| 3 | 30 | 10/10 (100%) | 10/10 (100%) | 0% |
   112	
   113	This result is bounded. The tests and answers were produced in the same
   114	pipeline, so the useful signal is the reported delta, not a general claim about
   115	model accuracy. The benchmark was not rerun for this revision.
   116	
   117	The growing task-list example also exposes the operational risk. Once a header
   118	contains aliases such as <code>s</code>, <code>sc</code>,
   119	<code>sp</code>, and <code>st</code>, an agent answering a question about
   120	T-209 must first recover the dictionary, then find the row and its columns.
   121	The source materials identify five failure modes:
   122	
   123	1. No dictionary leaves an alias such as <code>c</code> ambiguous.
   124	2. Domain priors can conflict with a tool's chosen alias.
   125	3. Different tools can reuse the same alias for different fields.
   126	4. Partial context eviction can leave an incomplete dictionary.
   127	5. Large aggregations add attention pressure even when the dictionary is present.
   128	
   129	An explicit dictionary prevents the first failure in the historical benchmark,
   130	but it creates the discovery and retention requirement evaluated next.
   131	
   132	## Study 3: The alias session model
   133	
   134	**Evidence: model estimate, not a runtime measurement.** The
   135	[session simulator](../.research/session-simulator/simulate.py) models an
   136	agent that refreshes its alias dictionary after every <em>K</em> turns. It
   137	hard-codes the historical 85-token schema roundtrip and 5-token compact-header
   138	saving, plus a query mix and eviction schedule. Those inputs are assumptions of
   139	the model; they are not observations of Codex, Claude, MCP, or another agent
   140	host.
   141	
   142	| Model input | Value used by the simulator | Evidence boundary |
   143	| --- | ---: | --- |
   144	| Schema roundtrip | 85 tok | Historical measurement reused as a model input |
   145	| Compact alias saving per query | 5 tok | Historical fixture result reused as a model input |
   146	| Context eviction and query mix | Scenario-specific | Assumption |
   147	
   148	With those inputs, the simulator produces these estimates:
   149	
   150	| Session queries | Eviction K | Schema calls | Schema cost | Alias savings | Net |
   151	| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
   152	| 10 | 10 | 1 | 85 | 40 | -45 |
   153	| 10 | never | 1 | 85 | 40 | -45 |
   154	| 20 | 10 | 2 | 170 | 80 | -90 |
   155	| 20 | 20 | 1 | 85 | 80 | -5 |
   156	| 50 | 10 | 5 | 425 | 200 | -225 |
   157	| 50 | 50 | 1 | 85 | 200 | +115 |
   158	| 100 | 10 | 10 | 850 | 400 | -450 |
   159	| 100 | 20 | 5 | 425 | 400 | -25 |
   160	| 100 | 50 | 2 | 170 | 400 | +230 |
   161	| 100 | never | 1 | 85 | 400 | +315 |
   162	
   163	For its compact-format cases, the simulator evaluates 16 cases and reports four
   164	positive outcomes. For example, its compact scenario for 20 queries with K=20
   165	returns -5, while 50 queries with K=50 returns +115. That result supports only
   166	this conditional statement: aliases can be positive when the model's discovery
   167	cost is amortized over sufficiently many remembered queries. It does not measure
   168	a typical agent session or a transport comparison.
   169	
   170	The simple all-data-query amortization check for the model's 85-token and
   171	5-token inputs is:
   172	
   173	~~~text
   174	queries_between_evictions > schema_cost / savings_per_query
   175	~~~
   176	
   177	At those inputs, one schema roundtrip requires 17 such data queries to
   178	amortize. This is a derived model value, not a host-independent threshold.
   179	
   180	### Batching is a separate implementation capability
   181	
   182	The alias model should not be turned into a claim that one transport universally
   183	beats another. Still, batching changes the local execution shape in a way the
   184	current implementation can verify.
   185	
   186	**Situation:** an agent needs the status of T-209, T-210, and T-211.
   187	**Expected behavior:** this DSL accepts semicolon-separated statements and
   188	returns the results in source order.
   189	
   190	~~~text
   191	get(T-209) { status }; get(T-210) { status }; get(T-211) { status }
   192	~~~
   193	
   194	The parser and executor implement this multi-statement behavior, and the test
   195	suite covers three batched statements and compact rendering. This shows a
   196	capability of this Go DSL. It does not supply a per-call token total, prove a
   197	particular host framing cost, or establish anything about an MCP server's
   198	batching behavior.
   199	
   200	## Discussion: choose the structural optimization first
   201	
   202	The historical fixtures support a narrow structural insight: a schema-once
   203	header removes repeated field names from the rows. Aliases can only shorten
   204	that one declaration. The simulator then models whether learning the aliases
   205	pays for that fixed saving under its stated assumptions.
   206	
   207	| Priority | Optimization | Evidence in this repository | Boundary |
   208	| ---: | --- | --- | --- |
   209	| 1 | Field projection | Implementation selects requested fields | No percentage is claimed here |
   210	| 2 | Compact schema-once output | Historical fixtures report 44.5% to 46.6% versus JSON | Fixture and tokenizer specific |
   211	| 3 | Semicolon batching | Verified implementation behavior | External call savings depend on host and transport |
   212	| 4 | Presets | Implementation feature for common selections | No token saving is asserted here |
   213	| 5 | Field aliases | Historical fixtures show a fixed 5-token compact-header delta | Dictionary discovery is modeled, not newly measured |
   214	
   215	### MCP comparison: scoped conclusion
   216	
   217	The accepted evidence map separates implementation facts from transport
   218	economics:
   219	
   220	| Question | What the evidence supports | What remains unknown |
   221	| --- | --- | --- |
   222	| Can this DSL batch requests? | Yes. The [parser](../agentquery/parser.go#L318-L369) accepts semicolon-separated statements and [QueryAST](../agentquery/query.go#L30-L60) executes a batch. | The token saving for a particular host or transport. |
   223	| Is compact output schema-once? | Yes. [FormatCompact](../agentquery/format.go#L9-L62) writes a field header before its rows. | A universal percentage across tokenizers and payloads. |
   224	| Is MCP more or less token-efficient here? | No repository-local result answers this. | There is no MCP adapter, tool-definition fixture, prompt snapshot, host trace, or aligned workload. |
   225	
   226	MCP batching and discovery costs must be bounded by protocol version, SDK,
   227	server design, and host behavior. The official
   228	[Ruby SDK protocol-version reference](https://ruby.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/protocol-versions/)
   229	records that protocol version 2025-06-18 removed JSON-RPC batching, while the
   230	official [TypeScript SDK request-body reference](https://ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/api/@modelcontextprotocol/server/server/requestBody.html)
   231	documents a current maximum of 100 messages in a JSON-RPC batch array. Neither
   232	source guarantees that an agent host exposes or sends a batch, and neither
   233	measures prompt-token cost for this repository.
   234	
   235	**Reasoning: MCP can still be the better interface when interoperability is the
   236	requirement rather than a proven local token minimum.** A remote service that
   237	must expose model-controlled tools to several MCP-capable hosts, or an existing
   238	MCP deployment that already meets the integration requirement, can justify MCP.
   239	The [MCP server overview](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/server/index)
   240	supports that tool-interoperability rationale. It is not evidence of a token
   241	win. The failure mode is treating any of those integration benefits as a
   242	measured break-even point; a real comparison needs a server/host pair, protocol
   243	and SDK version, discovery transcript, tokenizer, and aligned workload.
   244	
